    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(3))  # U0..U2\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[26.18], [19.14], [33.07]], [[38.95], [18.8], [15.63]], [[27.75], [27.49], [32.68]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[37.97], [29.53], [28.07]], [[39.01], [21.18], [23.27]], [[28.54], [21.13], [29.9]]]\n    transport_costs = [[4.81, 2.5, 3.17], [2.18, 3.94, 3.38], [3.23, 1.18, 2.74]]\n    risk_costs = [[186.58, 181.85, 108.34], [67.0, 57.77, 124.58], [171.36, 152.31, 108.49]]\n    supply_volumes = [1.31, 0.78, 1.52]\n    penalty_costs = [75.57, 56.07, 62.86]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe storm system has disrupted transport corridors and displaced populations across the operating area. Supply Depot Alpha (airfield logistics hub), Supply Depot Bravo (rail-linked warehouse), and Supply Depot Charlie (port storage complex) hold mixed stocks of priority commodities. The 1st Field Hospital (urban trauma center), the 3rd Relief Battalion (mountain valley IDP sites), and the Coastal Shelter Cluster (littoral camps) require immediate resupply within one operational day. Several corridors remain degraded; the river valley route is intermittently cut by debris, the mountain pass is exposed to rockfall and ambush risk, and the coastal highway has landslide hazards. Movement control will employ only road movements for this cycle.\n\nMission:\nDeliver critical supplies to the three supported units within the current 24-hour window while minimizing total operational cost. Costs include per-unit transport, penalties for unmet demand, per-vehicle deployment, and fixed security/clearance risk costs for any route that is activated.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Source and demand: Outbound shipments from each depot must not exceed on-hand stocks by supply type. Each receiving unit\u2019s demand by supply type should be met; any shortfall will be recorded as unmet demand and assessed a penalty per unit.\n- Mobility and capacity: Movements will be executed via depot-to-unit road routes. The total payload dispatched on a route is constrained by vehicle capacity and the number of vehicles assigned to that specific route. Payload calculations will account for the volume characteristics of each supply type to ensure no vehicle exceeds capacity.\n- Route activation and risk costs: A route is considered inactive until deliberately activated. Activating a route (sending any vehicles, even a single truck) incurs a fixed Risk Cost that represents escort teams, route clearance, and security overwatch. This fixed Risk Cost is applied once per route per day and is independent of the number of vehicles or tonnage that subsequently use the route. Vehicles may only travel on routes that have been activated.\n- Fleet management: The total number of vehicles dispatched across all routes cannot exceed the available fleet for the day. Each deployed vehicle also incurs a per-vehicle cost.\n- Cost objective: The plan will select flows (by depot, unit, and supply), determine how many vehicles to dispatch on each active route, and decide which routes to activate so as to minimize the sum of transport costs, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route Risk Costs, subject to the above constraints.\n- Priorities: Life-saving commodities to the 1st Field Hospital and critical water and shelter items to population centers will be prioritized where penalty avoidance and clinical impact warrant. Given the fixed Risk Cost of activation, only routes whose activation yields net cost benefits (through reduced penalties and/or lower transport costs) will be opened. High-capacity corridors will be favored to reduce vehicle counts, provided security and terrain allow.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  26.18 |  19.14 |  33.07\nU 1        |  38.95 |  18.80 |  15.63\nU 2        |  27.75 |  27.49 |  32.68\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  37.97 |  29.53 |  28.07\nD 1        |  39.01 |  21.18 |  23.27\nD 2        |  28.54 |  21.13 |  29.90\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        |   4.81 |   2.50 |   3.17\nD 1        |   2.18 |   3.94 |   3.38\nD 2        |   3.23 |   1.18 |   2.74\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2\n----------------------------\nD 0        | 186.58 | 181.85 | 108.34\nD 1        |  67.00 |  57.77 | 124.58\nD 2        | 171.36 | 152.31 | 108.49\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 75.57, Supply S1: 56.07, Supply S2: 62.86]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 1.31, Supply S1: 0.78, Supply S2: 1.52]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe storm system has disrupted transport corridors and displaced populations across the operating area. Supply Depot Alpha (airfield logistics hub), Supply Depot Bravo (rail-linked warehouse), and Supply Depot Charlie (port storage complex) hold mixed stocks of priority commodities. The 1st Field Hospital (urban trauma center), the 3rd Relief Battalion (mountain valley IDP sites), and the Coastal Shelter Cluster (littoral camps) require immediate resupply within one operational day. Several corridors remain degraded; the river valley route is intermittently cut by debris, the mountain pass is exposed to rockfall and ambush risk, and the coastal highway has landslide hazards. Movement control will employ only road movements for this cycle.\n\nMission:\nDeliver critical supplies to the three supported units within the current 24-hour window while minimizing total operational cost. Costs include per-unit transport, penalties for unmet demand, per-vehicle deployment, and fixed security/clearance risk costs for any route that is activated.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Source and demand: Outbound shipments from each depot must not exceed on-hand stocks by supply type. Each receiving unit\u2019s demand by supply type should be met; any shortfall will be recorded as unmet demand and assessed a penalty per unit.\n- Mobility and capacity: Movements will be executed via depot-to-unit road routes. The total payload dispatched on a route is constrained by vehicle capacity and the number of vehicles assigned to that specific route. Payload calculations will account for the volume characteristics of each supply type to ensure no vehicle exceeds capacity.\n- Route activation and risk costs: A route is considered inactive until deliberately activated. Activating a route (sending any vehicles, even a single truck) incurs a fixed Risk Cost that represents escort teams, route clearance, and security overwatch. This fixed Risk Cost is applied once per route per day and is independent of the number of vehicles or tonnage that subsequently use the route. Vehicles may only travel on routes that have been activated.\n- Fleet management: The total number of vehicles dispatched across all routes cannot exceed the available fleet for the day. Each deployed vehicle also incurs a per-vehicle cost.\n- Cost objective: The plan will select flows (by depot, unit, and supply), determine how many vehicles to dispatch on each active route, and decide which routes to activate so as to minimize the sum of transport costs, shortage penalties, vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route Risk Costs, subject to the above constraints.\n- Priorities: Life-saving commodities to the 1st Field Hospital and critical water and shelter items to population centers will be prioritized where penalty avoidance and clinical impact warrant. Given the fixed Risk Cost of activation, only routes whose activation yields net cost benefits (through reduced penalties and/or lower transport costs) will be opened. High-capacity corridors will be favored to reduce vehicle counts, provided security and terrain allow.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
